Reset, instead of accepting, a TCP connection that an app outside the VPN
pushes into the tun on Android, behind keqdroid's outside-vpn rule.

An app excluded from the VPN can still bind a socket to tun0 (SO_BINDTODEVICE
needs no rights since Linux 5.7). keqdroid writes a first rule, tagged
outside-vpn, that blocks connections the app names as outside the VPN (see
xray-android-uid-owner.patch and ConnectionOwnerServer.kt). But the gVisor
stack completes the TCP handshake before routing runs, so such an app saw a
connection that opened and then closed: proof that the tun answers.

proxy/tun now asks the router for that one rule before the handshake
(Router.MatchRuleTag applies only the rule with the given tag, nothing above
it) and answers a match with a reset. No outside-vpn rule in the config means
no lookup and no change. The owner lookup goes through the finder's two
second cache, so the routing that follows does not ask the app again. UDP has
no handshake and keeps being dropped by the rule itself.

Android only (owner_guard_android.go); other platforms get a stub.

Built by tool/build_xray_cores.ps1. Checked against xray-core v26.10.10.

